  "query_preview": "(Today through Thursday)...\nIssued at 316 AM EDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nRidging continues for most of central Indiana as well as heat and \nhumidity. A heat advisory remains in effect for today as highs will \nremain in the 90s and may even see 100 degrees in our far SW. Heat \nindices today will again reach near to above 105. Tomorrow should be \na few degrees cooler at least with more of a temperature gradient \nacross the forecast area but only moderate confidence on exact highs \nas it will largely depend on how convection plays out today. \n\nA weak boundary sits along the periphery of the ridge, north and \neast of the forecast area, while troughing to the NE continues to \ntry to nudge its way in. This is leading to uncertainty in PoPs over \nthe coming days as it is still unclear when the high pressure will \nfinally let up enough. CAMs continue to hold off on the boundary and \nassociated precipitation from creeping in today, but isolated \nshowers and storms are still probable periodically throughout the \nday. Chances for isolated to scattered storms should see better \nchances as we get into Saturday, assuming the boundary is finally \nable to dip further SE. Still can't rule out a few strong to severe \nstorms should storms actually form within central Indiana, but this \nthreat remains conditions. \n\nHolding onto the upper high further retrograding late this weekend \ninto early next week, with which a backdoor cold front should \nfinally bring some relief to the heat and humidity, at least for a \ncouple of days. By midweek, the upper ridging is expected to return \nand with it a brief warming trend. Models are showing that the upper \npattern should become more zonal as next week progresses which \nshould allow the heat to relax again. Still watching trends for \nadditional precipitation chances sometime mid to late next week.",
